  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 AM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nA stubborn low stratus deck persists across far northeast TN and \nsouthwest VA. RAP soundings suggest this will gradually clear out \nover the next several hours, with mostly sunny skies expected today. \nAdditionally, H5 heights will be increasing nearly ~12-14dam as the \nupper level trough responsible for this seasonally cool weather is \nfinally kicked northeastward by ridging to our west. The upper \nheight rises will translate to near normal afternoon MaxTs in the \nupper 50s to low 60s for most. Highest peaks in the mountains may \nstruggle to reach 50, however. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to dominate and provide dry weather \nthrough the latter half of the week, all while the aforementioned \nridge centers over the southeastern CONUS promoting above normal \ntemperatures. By the end of the work week the afternoon temperatures \nwill be around 5 to 10 degrees above normal; or closer to that of \nmid/late October standards. \n\nThere are some model solutions that show some rain returning to our \neastern fringes next weekend, however, given the very poor model \nagreement/run to run consistency, and the usual long range timing \nuncertainties, the ensemble approach of the NBM, which keeps us \nmainly dry through the forecast period, looks reasonable. Depending \nhow the system pans out, minor upper height falls could also lead to \ntemperatures closer to seasonal normals by the end of the \nweekend/early next week.",
